## Counting How Many Times Each Item Appears

A **frequency counter** uses a Map where keys are items and values are counts. For each item in the input, increment its count in the Map.

### Building the Counter

```xiom
fn count_frequencies(items: &Vec[Str]) -> Map[Str, Int] {
  let counts: Map[Str, Int] = Map[Str, Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    let item = match items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => "" };
    let current = match counts.get(&item) { Some(c) => c, None => 0 };
    counts.insert(item, current + 1);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return counts;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let votes: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  votes.push("Alice"); votes.push("Bob");
  votes.push("Alice"); votes.push("Carol");
  votes.push("Alice"); votes.push("Bob");

  let tally = count_frequencies(&votes);

  let alice_count = match tally.get(&"Alice") { Some(c) => c, None => 0 };
  io.println("Alice: " + string.int_to_str(alice_count));
  return 0;
}
```

### Finding the Winner

```xiom
fn find_winner(counts: &Map[Str, Int], candidates: &Vec[Str]) -> Str {
  let winner = "";
  let max = 0;
  let i = 0;
  while i < candidates.len() {
    let name = match candidates.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    let count = match counts.get(&name) { Some(c) => c, None => 0 };
    if count > max {
      max = count;
      winner = name;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return winner;
}
```

### Finding Least Common

Exactly the same pattern, but look for the minimum:

```xiom
fn find_least_common(counts: &Map[Str, Int], candidates: &Vec[Str]) -> Str {
  let least = "";
  let min = 999999;
  let i = 0;
  while i < candidates.len() {
    let name = match candidates.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    let count = match counts.get(&name) { Some(c) => c, None => 999999 };
    if count < min {
      min = count;
      least = name;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return least;
}
```

The core pattern is: Map[Item, Count], iterate to increment, scan to find max/min.

